WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.161
Hey everybody, it's Craig Thomas.
大家好，我是克雷格·托马斯。

00:00:02.161 --> 00:00:10.839
I wrote a book and I want you to know about it, but instead of watching me or listening to me be self-promotional in a blatant and uncomfortable way.
我写了一本书，想让大家知道，但与其看我或听我进行那种厚颜无耻又让人尴尬的自我宣传……

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:13.507
I was delighted by this book.
我对这本书爱不释手。

00:00:13.507 --> 00:00:15.623
It is super page-turny.
它真的超级吸引人，让人一页接一页停不下来。

00:00:15.623 --> 00:00:20.039
It is like breathlessly and hilariously plotted.
它的情节设计既扣人心弦又充满喜感。

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:25.879
The interiority was just as rich and as fun as the plot twists.
角色的内心世界和情节反转一样丰富有趣。

00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:31.079
His many years of crafting great narratives on television came to bear on this.
他多年来在电视领域创作精彩叙事的功底在这本书里得到了充分体现。

00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:33.759
Your gifts as a novelist are just abundant.
你作为小说家的天赋简直太丰沛了。

00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:35.240
I was so delighted by it.
我真的太喜欢它了。

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.920
It's incredibly vivid.
它极其生动。

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.200
You're really seeing it as you read it.
你在阅读时仿佛历历在目。

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.920
Even if you were my mortal enemy, I would be forced to.
哪怕你是我的死敌，我也会被迫……

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.759
I'd love you dearly.
我会深深地爱你。

00:00:42.759 --> 00:00:47.399
You're one of my best friends, but I would be forced to concede that you had written a great book.
你是我最好的朋友之一，但我还是不得不承认你写了一本好书。

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:48.369
That is amazing.
这太惊人了。

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:53.399
I wish that could be a blurb on the book, even if Frank Thomas was my mortal enemy.
我真希望这能成为书上的推荐语，哪怕弗兰克·托马斯是我的死敌。

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:59.000
You will not be disappointed if you pick up this book or listen to this book, so check it out.
如果你买这本书或者听这本书，你绝对不会失望，快去看看吧。

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.454
And speaking of listening to this book, there is a mini How I Met Your Mother reunion in the audiobook of That's Not How It Happened.
说起听这本书，在《那是怎么发生的》的有声书中，有一个迷你的《老爸老妈浪漫史》重聚。

00:01:06.454 --> 00:01:07.799
Listen to Kobe Smoltres.
听听寇碧·史莫德斯怎么说。

00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:15.799
And so began the surreal, unholy process of my husband adapting my book, My Life, into a screenplay.
于是，我丈夫开始了我将我的书《我的一生》改编成剧本的超现实、神圣而又诡异的过程。

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:19.480
Here's another voice you may recognize, Josh Redder.
这里还有另一个你可能会听出来的声音，乔希·拉德纳。

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.706
Right now, I was in that pocket.
当时，我正处于那种状态中。

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:25.879
And as a writer, that's about as close to heaven as it gets.
作为一名作家，这几乎就是天堂般的享受了。

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.484
Okay, self-promotion over.
好了，自我宣传到此结束。

00:01:28.484 --> 00:01:30.359
Now to this week's episode.
现在进入本周的节目。

00:01:30.359 --> 00:01:31.400
It's a big one.
这期分量很重。

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.079
Ladies and gentlemen, how we met Neil Patrick Harris.
女士们先生们，我们是如何认识尼尔·帕特里克·哈里斯的。

00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:55.739
me to tell you everything in New York City.
让我把纽约市的一切都告诉你。

00:01:55.739 --> 00:02:00.540
Hello and welcome to another episode of How We Made Your Mother.
大家好，欢迎收听另一期的《老爸老妈浪漫史幕后故事》。

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.260
I'm Josh Ratner, your co-host, my other co-host.
我是乔希·拉德纳，你的联合主持人，还有我的另一位联合主持人。

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.700
I'm staring at his hands and face.
我正盯着他的手和脸。

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.819
Now his name's Craig Thomas.
现在他的名字叫克雷格·托马斯。

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:07.579
Hi, Craig.
嗨，克雷格。

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:07.980
Why?
为什么？

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.020
I'm blushing, sir.
我脸红了，先生。

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.099
Thank you.
谢谢你。

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:11.340
Do I not call you handsome enough?
难道是我平时夸你不够帅吗？

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.419
Not really enough.
其实不太够。

00:02:12.419 --> 00:02:13.620
It's implied.
都在不言中。

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.900
I'm always feeling it.
我一直能感受到。

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.780
We made a TV show together years ago.
我们多年前一起制作了一部电视剧。

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.020
It started what, 20, 19 years ago.
它开始于，呃，20年前，19年前。

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:22.620
It was called How I Met Your Mother.
它叫《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.060
We did 208 episodes.
我们拍了208集。

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.699
It ran from 2005 to 2014.
它从2005年一直播到2014年。

00:02:26.699 --> 00:02:27.939
We haven't seen it in years.
我们已经好多年没看过了。

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:32.740
We're going back through every single episode with our older, wiser eyes.
我们要带着如今更年长、更睿智的目光重新回顾每一集。

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.539
We're very proud of what we made for the most part.
大部分情况下，我们为我们所创作的内容感到非常骄傲。

00:02:35.539 --> 00:02:37.860
And yeah, we're having a great time.
而且是的，我们度过了一段非常美好的时光。

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.819
And we always say this is a special episode.
我们总是说这是一集特别节目。

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:41.939
This is a special episode.
这是一集特别节目。

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:53.860
This isn't just such a special extra, extra special episode Because we have a man here who, it's just impossible to imagine the world of how I met your mother without.
这绝不仅仅是一集普通的、额外特别的节目，因为我们今天请到了一位嘉宾——没有他，简直无法想象《老爸老妈浪漫史》的世界。

00:02:53.860 --> 00:03:01.620
He is the person who breathed life into the great iconic Barney Stinson and his name is Neil Patrick Harrison.
他就是为伟大而标志性的巴尼·斯廷森赋予生命的人，他的名字叫尼尔·帕特里克·哈里斯。

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.620
He's with us today.
他今天和我们在一起。

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.939
What?
什么？

00:03:03.939 --> 00:03:04.939
What?
什么？

00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:05.780
Hey, Neil.
嗨，尼尔。

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.979
Please sit down, please, everyone sit.
请坐，请坐，大家都坐。

00:03:07.979 --> 00:03:11.060
What's up, man?
最近怎么样，老兄？

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:11.900
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:12.740
Good to see you.
很高兴见到你。

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.900
It's nice to see your smiling faces.
很高兴看到你们笑脸相迎。

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.360
Oh my gosh, Alec as well.
哦天哪，还有亚历克。

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.139
Thank you for being here, buddy.
谢谢你能来，兄弟。

00:03:19.139 --> 00:03:21.979
What have you been doing since 2014?
自2014年以来你都在忙些什么？

00:03:21.979 --> 00:03:22.939
How have you been?
你最近过得怎么样？

00:03:24.539 --> 00:03:26.139
I've been good, hookin'.
我过得很好，一直在接活。

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:29.139
I saw you, I saw your, you won a Tony, I saw that.
我看到你了，我看到你，你赢了一项托尼奖，我看到了。

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:33.219
I remember you jumped on my seat in the audience and you kissed me, I believe on the mouth.
我记得你当时从观众席跳到了我的座位上，而且我记得你亲了我的嘴。

00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:34.060
For sure.
绝对有。

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.139
I think I got glitter on my face.
我想我的脸上还沾上了亮片。

00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:37.620
Well, we had already, we'll talk about that.
好吧，我们已经……我们等会儿会聊到这个。

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.819
We had already broken that.
我们当时已经突破那个界限了。

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.939
And so you're living in New York City And let me just start with this.
所以你现在住在纽约市，让我先从这个聊起吧。

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:54.740
We talk about how the tale of this show, there were shows that were so popular when we were on the air, that people just do not talk about anymore.
我们经常谈到这部剧的传奇色彩，当我们还在播出的时有些剧非常火爆，但现在人们根本不再谈论它们了。

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:59.240
But for some reason, How I Met Your Mother is still very much in the zeitgeist, it's still in the culture.
但不知为什么，《老爸老妈浪漫史》依然深深烙印在流行文化中，它仍然活在文化里。

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.699
People still reference it, talk about it, discover it anew.
人们仍然在引用它、谈论它、并不断发现新的乐趣。

00:04:02.699 --> 00:04:10.620
So I'm just curious, in your day-to-day life, you get recognized for a lot of things, but what's your experience of meeting how I met your mother fans?
所以我很好奇，在你的日常生活中，你因为很多事情被认出来，但你和《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝见面时的体验是怎样的？

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:18.860
and how has the show kind of changed in your perception of it as you've gotten more time away from it?
随着你与这部剧的距离越来越远，你对它的看法发生了怎样的变化？

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.779
Wow, an interesting question.
哇，这是一个很有趣的问题。

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:34.139
It's been constant, I mean, really, truly constant because when we did our final season, the show was on more times per day than ever.
这种经历一直都在，我是说，真的，实实在在地一直都在，因为在我们拍最后一季的时候，这部剧每天播出的次数比以往任何时候都要多。

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:37.899
So it was on three or four times a day in America.
它在美国一天要播三到四次。

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:46.259
And then when Netflix picked it up, it had another resurgence, and then it went somewhere else, then Netflix picked it up again, it had another resurgence.
然后当奈飞买下它时，它又迎来了又一次复苏；接着它去了别的地方，奈飞又把它买回去，它又迎来了又一次复苏。

00:04:46.259 --> 00:04:56.980
Now, you know, I have my husband and I have twin 14-year-old children and their friends are watching it now, they're from high school.
现在呢，你知道的，我和我丈夫有14岁的双胞胎孩子，他们的高中同学现在也在看这部剧。

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.220
Are they watching it at Harper and Gideon watch it?
哈珀和吉迪恩也在看吗？

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.860
Yeah, for sure, so.
对，肯定的，所以。

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:00.699
Oh my God.
哦天哪。

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:25.339
So that's interesting because now that's a whole other level of like celebrity dynamic, but in a strange direction, because, you know, I want Harper and Gideon to be identified independently and they have their own, you know, triggers and issues with potentially only being recognized as the child of Barney Stinson.
所以这很有趣，因为现在这达到了另一种名人生态的层面，但却是一个奇怪的方向，因为，你知道的，我希望哈珀和吉迪恩能被独立地认可，他们有自己的，你知道的，潜在的心理负担和困扰，那就是只被当作巴尼·斯廷森的孩子来认出。

00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:27.740
It's a complicated legacy.
这是一个复杂的遗产。

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:28.740
Yeah, for sure.
是的，确实如此。

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.339
It's a good and bad and it's something that they'll have to process.
它有好有坏，这也是他们以后需要去面对和消化的东西。

00:05:32.339 --> 00:05:45.660
But I've had some really great marker chapters professionally in my life since then that have been super great and recognizable and fulfilling as well.
但从那以后，我在职业生涯中确实经历了一些非常棒的重要阶段，它们同样非常精彩、广为人知且充实。

00:05:45.660 --> 00:06:04.877
But boy, yeah, people keep coming back to Barney and I'm so appreciative of it and I'm so grateful for the show because especially having played Barney, he was intended to be kind of the fifth wheel life of the party guy that you want to buy a drink and buy the next round and go on some crazy acid venture, right?
不过天哪，是的，人们总是不断谈论巴尼，我对此非常感激，我也非常感谢这部剧，尤其是扮演了巴尼这个角色——他本来被设定为那种“电灯泡”、聚会上的灵魂人物，是你想要请他喝一杯、再请下一轮、一起去进行某种疯狂的迷幻冒险的人？

00:06:04.877 --> 00:06:08.060
That was how he was going to construct it, it seems.
看来剧本一开始是这样构思他的。

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:30.582
And so when I meet people who love the show, they're very positive and they're very enthusiastic and they have specifics and they tell me about the playbook and how many that they tried and that they met their girl doing the naked man and that they've been to the Liberty Bell, like, you know, it's, and a lot of people say that they have watched the show eight times.
所以当我遇到喜欢这部剧的人时，他们都非常积极、充满热情，而且他们能说出具体细节，还会跟我讲“情圣宝典”（The Playbook）、他们尝试过多少招式、他们通过“赤裸男”（The Naked Man）追到了他们的女孩、他们去过自由钟……你知道的，而且很多人说他们已经把这部剧看了八遍。

00:06:30.582 --> 00:06:31.082
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:31.082 --> 00:06:32.306
All the way through.
从头到尾刷完。

00:06:32.306 --> 00:06:38.855
Do you ever have the experience that they're bringing up something that you're like, I don't remember that.
你有没有过这种经历：他们提起某个桥段，而你的反应是“我不记得了”？

00:06:38.855 --> 00:06:43.873
Like they're they did something so specific, like they just watched it last night.
就像他们说得极其具体，仿佛他们昨晚才刚看过一样。

00:06:43.873 --> 00:06:47.607
And you're like, I barely remember what you're talking about.
而你的反应是：我根本记不清你在说什么了。

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:48.158
For sure.
那是肯定的。

00:06:48.158 --> 00:06:55.073
And and especially when someone comes up and they were on in the show, in an episode that had four or five lines.
特别是当有人走过来，而他们曾在剧中客串过，在只有四五句台词的一集里出现过。

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.800
You had a really awkward thing with Joe Manganello, you're like, I don't know this guy.
你和乔·曼根尼罗之间有过一件非常尴尬的事，你当时说，我不认识这家伙。

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.439
I haven't seen you before in my life.
我这辈子都没见过你。

00:07:02.439 --> 00:07:03.439
But wow, look at your arms.
但是哇，看看你的胳膊。

00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:05.600
And he takes his shirt off and you're like, oh, I remember this guy.
然后他脱下衬衫，你就说，哦，我想起这家伙了。

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:06.439
Oh yeah, that guy.
哦对，就是那家伙。

00:07:08.079 --> 00:07:12.040
So no, it's just been, it's just been a delight.
所以，不，这真的，真的太令人高兴了。

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:15.399
It weirdly, I'm 52 years old, you know?
说来也怪，我都52岁了，你知道吗？

00:07:15.399 --> 00:07:26.079
And people still consider me, will treat me as if I'm like vibrant, let's go have a great time and do fun things together.
人们依然认为我很，把我当成那种充满活力的人，我们去好好玩玩，一起做点有趣的事吧。

00:07:26.079 --> 00:07:27.079
Yeah.
对。

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:27.920
And that's fun.
这很有趣。

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:36.920
Like it's fun to not be like the dusty older sage, but instead to still be like the one that can be devilish.
就像不用扮演那个古板的老智者，而是依然能做一个调皮捣蛋的人，这很有趣。

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.720
It was a devilish part.
那是一个很调皮的角色。

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:46.240
And I'm so grateful, Craig, to you and Carter, mostly to you, just because Carter's not here right now.
克雷格，我非常感谢你和卡特，主要是感谢你，只是因为卡特现在不在这里。

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.720
The feeling is very mutual.
我的感受完全一样。

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.120
Neil, I don't want to, I'm struggling with how much are a little too embarrassed to you during this.
尼尔，我不想，我正在纠结在节目里对你表现得是不是有点太害羞了。

00:07:52.120 --> 00:08:02.399
But like each, every single episode we've done and we're at the end of season two almost now, every week we're saying like, how the fuck did Neil do that physical comedy thing or how did you find that moment?
但我们拍的每一集——现在几乎快到第二季末了——每周我们都在说，尼尔他妈的到底是怎么做到那种肢体喜剧的，或者你是怎么找到那个灵感的？

00:08:02.399 --> 00:08:05.639
How, how does he do that turn on a dime the way he does?
他怎么能做到像他那样说变就变？

00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:09.879
There's, we're, we're, we're fanning out on you a bunch rewatching our own show that we all made together.
我们，我们，我们都在为你着迷，一遍又一遍地重温我们大家一起制作的自己的节目。

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:10.879
So I just wanted to say that.
所以我只是想说这个。

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:11.879
Thank you, man.
谢谢你，老兄。

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:17.680
It was, it was one of those rare gifts that I'm sure you have talked about ad nauseam.
这，这是那种罕见的天赋之一，我确信你们已经翻来覆去地讨论过了。

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:24.480
where it was such a hybrid show at a time when that really didn't exist.
在那个这种形式并不真正存在的时代，它是一个如此融合的节目。

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:43.740
And so we were doing a multi-camera show with no audience as if there was an audience, and we were able to cut and reset and restart, and we were helmed by Pam Fryman, who was such like a heartbeat truthful sensor for everyone in their own ways, always.
所以我们在没有观众的情况下拍摄多机位节目，就好像有观众一样，我们能够剪辑、重置和重新开始，而且我们由帕姆·弗莱曼掌舵，她在各自的方面一直是每个人的心跳和真实感应器，始终如此。

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:48.803
And that North Star, I think, allowed everyone freedom to try shit and fail.
我认为，那个北极星般的指引让每个人都有自由去尝试各种胡闹并经历失败。

00:08:48.803 --> 00:09:02.862
And the stuff that, I mean, I just felt like it was this back and forth between team writers room and team actor is they would sort of challenge us with like what, let's see if they, if this would be successful.
我的意思是，我觉得编剧团队和演员团队之间有一种来回的互动，他们会用诸如“让我们看看他们能不能成功”之类的事情来挑战我们。

00:09:02.862 --> 00:10:00.628
And when it came to the Barney stuff, because, you know, Josh was so exquisite at the, at the pathos part and at making a laugh-tracked 22 minutes feel so sort of cinematic and real that then I was sort of gifted all of like the craziness and I was so fun to do because if it was a real sitcom with a multi-camera live studio audience it's a very different experience you rehearse all week and then it's show night and you really only have a couple cracks at it you do it and then you huddle up and the audience laughed at it, but they didn't laugh like you thought, so you write a bunch of stuff and then the guy is upstairs telling them, I'll give you chocolates if you laugh louder, and so then the second time you do it, it's like really big responses for everything and they're wanting to hear themselves when they go home later, so that's just a different vibe.
而当涉及到巴ney的桥段时，因为，你知道，乔什在表现悲情以及让带有罐头笑声的22分钟充满电影感和真实感方面是如此出色，所以我仿佛得到了所有疯狂桥段的天赋，演起来真的太有趣了。因为如果这是一个有现场多机位观众的真正情景喜剧，那将是完全不同的体验：你排练一整周，然后是演出之夜，你其实只有几次机会，你演完，然后大家聚在一起，观众笑了，但他们笑得和你想象的不一样，所以你重写了一堆东西，然后楼上的人告诉他们：“如果你笑得更大声，我就给你巧克力”，于是你第二次演的时候，所有东西都有了非常热烈的反应，他们希望以后回家时能听到自己的声音，所以这完全是另一种氛围。

00:10:00.628 --> 00:10:03.559
We were able to do things five or six times.
我们能够把事情做五六遍。

00:10:03.559 --> 00:10:04.919
And just trust what we felt.
并且只需相信我们的感觉。

00:10:04.919 --> 00:10:06.134
Did we think it was good?
我们觉得它好吗？

00:10:06.134 --> 00:10:07.639
Did our crew think it was good?
我们的剧组觉得它好吗？

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:13.299
It was we were the arbiters and I wanted to make the crew laugh was was paramount.
我们就是裁判，而我想逗笑剧组则是重中之重。

00:10:13.299 --> 00:10:20.600
My favorite person to try and make laugh was Sue, our editor, because I know that she was the one pouring over all this stuff.
我最想逗笑的人是苏，我们的剪辑师，因为我知道她是那个字斟句酌地研究所有这些素材的人。

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:27.480
So I was I was I was constantly when we were filming trying to do little weird things that only she would acknowledge.
所以我，所以我，当我们在拍摄时，经常会做一些只有她才能注意到的古怪小动作。

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:33.480
She got the she had a whole relationship with you and I had a whole making it me.
她得到了，她和你建立了一种完整的关系，而我也在建立我自己的关系。

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:44.399
I feel weird that not for any other reason than I just could only imagine it's boring to be seeing like, which take do I do of these same similar five takes or something.
我觉得有点奇怪，原因无他，我只是觉得看这些相似的五个镜头到底用哪一个一定很无聊。

00:10:44.399 --> 00:10:46.679
So I was just doing runner bits for her.
所以我只是在为她做一些连贯的小段子。

00:10:46.679 --> 00:10:48.840
That was like the playful spirit that you brought to it.
你带给它的就是那种俏皮的精神。

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:55.240
You kind of didn't go out of character in between takes and Sue would watch, like not even takes just going like, what the fuck's Neil doing over here?
你在镜头之间甚至都不会出戏，苏会看着，甚至都不是在拍镜头，只是说：“尼尔在这儿他妈的在干什么？”

00:10:55.240 --> 00:11:00.320
I think he's doing this just for me, but I think some of that energy makes it like that is the energy.
我想他这么做只是为了我，但我想这种能量的一部分让它变成了——那就是那种能量。

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:05.740
the energy of the weird little like mad scientist laboratory of making the show together.
一起制作节目的那个古怪的小疯狂科学家实验室的能量。

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:06.775
It's really cool.
真的很酷。

00:11:06.775 --> 00:11:13.474
When you guys would write something like Barney sneezes the biggest sneeze that he's ever sneezed in his life.
当你们写出类似“巴ney打了一个他这辈子打过的最大喷嚏”这样的情节时。

00:11:13.474 --> 00:11:14.753
That's all you'd say.
这就是你们说的全部。

00:11:14.753 --> 00:11:16.762
I was like, oh, I love this game.
我就想，哦，我喜欢这个游戏。

00:11:16.762 --> 00:11:17.920
Challenge accepted.
接受挑战。

00:11:17.919 --> 00:11:19.914
I mean, literal challenge accepted.
我是说，字面意义上的接受挑战。

00:11:19.914 --> 00:11:33.480
Neil, I remember we, when we watched episode three, Sweet Taste of Liberty, which was written by Miller and Lord, and Craig said that they said to him, What reminded me, Craig, they said this is where Barney is like an animated character.
尼尔，我记得我们，当我们看第三集《自由的甜美滋味》时，那是由米勒和洛德编剧的，克雷格说他们对他说过——这让我想起了什么，克雷格，他们说这就是巴ney像个动画角色的时候。

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:39.299
They said, we're going to make Barney a cartoon character in this one, and you don't want to burn it.
他们说，我们要在这一集里把巴ney变成一个卡通角色，而你不会搞砸的。

00:11:39.299 --> 00:11:48.919
That wouldn't be every episode, but to do it in that one, the third episode of the entire series to say, this is a Barney cartoon adventure, trust us where Chris and Phil, we will make this fantastic.
这不会是每一集都有的，但在那一集里做这件事——整个系列的第三集——就像是在说，这是一场巴ney的卡通冒险，相信我们，克里斯和菲尔，我们会把它变得棒极了。

00:11:48.919 --> 00:11:49.919
And that was so good.
而那真是太棒了。

00:11:49.919 --> 00:11:51.279
So early, they called that shot.
他们那么早就定下了这个基调。

00:11:51.279 --> 00:11:52.279
They really brought it in.
他们真的把它实现了。

00:11:52.279 --> 00:12:01.840
I remember there was a moment where I was standing on the street and you pull up, I think in a limo or a tattoo, you pull up in a taxi and you were just like, get in, you know, you're demanding that I get in.
我记得有个时刻我站在街上，你把车开过来，我想是一辆豪华轿车或是一辆出租车，你把车开过来，然后你就说，上车，你知道的，你要求我上车。

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:08.639
And there was a look in your eye that was like new, that I was like, oh, Neil's dropped in like to another level of this character.
你的眼神里有一种类似于尼尔的东西，让我觉得，哦，尼尔已经进入了这个角色的另一个层级。

00:12:08.639 --> 00:12:18.559
Like, yeah, like I just remember like you knew, you were about to take Ted, but you were about to take the episode and like make it insane.
就像，是的，就像我只记得你当时心知肚明，你即将带上特德，但你要把这一集推向疯狂。

00:12:18.559 --> 00:12:23.679
And I just, that was the one, we are international businessmen on a business trip.
我只是，那是关键所在，我们是去出差的国际商人。

00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:24.519
You remember that?
你还记得吗？

00:12:24.519 --> 00:12:40.799
Yeah, yeah, which kind of like there's certain episodes we've noticed are like hope and Crosby road movies, like Old Timey, Walter Mathow, Jack Lemmon, kind of like just old timey buddy comedy movies.
对，对，就像我们注意到某些集数有点像霍普和克罗斯比的公路电影，有点像老派的沃尔特·马修、杰克·莱蒙那种老式的哥们喜剧电影。

00:12:40.799 --> 00:13:11.159
But I just wanna also shout out that even if you conceived of yourself as like fifth guy party guy, you know, one thing that I really loved that because we got so long to do it that Craig and Carter did, they gave me some like insane animated shit to do, but they also gave you some real heart forward stuff that was just as moving as any other characters, as you get deeper in with your father issues with, we just look at the episode, oh no, is this?
但我还想大声称赞的是，即使你把自己设想成第五个派对狂人，你知道，我真的很喜欢的一点是，因为我们有很长的时间来做这件事，克雷格和卡特让我去做一些疯狂的动画烂摊子，但他们也给了你一些真正走心的东西，和任何其他角色一样感人，随着你和父亲之间的问题越来越深入，我们来看看那一集，哦不，是这一集吗？

00:13:11.159 --> 00:13:12.000
Oh yeah, showdown.
哦对，摊牌。

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.320
In theory, we're talking about showdown.
理论上，我们在谈论摊牌。

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.320
At some point, we'll name check it and this comes in.
在某个时刻，我们会点出它的名字，然后这个情节就出现了。

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:25.840
The end of showdown when you reveal how Barney actually lost his virginity, but then he prefers the fantasy of that it was dirty dancing or whatever.
《摊牌》的结尾，当你透露巴ney实际上是怎么失去童贞的，但他更喜欢那是《辣身舞》之类的幻想。

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:26.679
Right, right, right.
对，对，对。

00:13:26.679 --> 00:13:27.519
Yeah.
是的。

00:13:27.519 --> 00:13:32.759
There's something so sad and moving but extra hilarious about like the tragedy at the heart of this man.
在这个男人的内心的悲剧背后，有些东西是如此悲伤和感人，却又格外滑稽。

00:13:32.759 --> 00:13:38.720
And we talk a lot about, you know, Shannon and the Peace Corps and how Barney became Barney.
我们聊了很多，你知道，关于香农和和平队，以及巴ney是如何成为巴ney的。

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:49.919
But underneath it, he's just like a scared kid who didn't have a male role model in his life who's just like trying a piece together this like way to be a man in a way that's just kind of like insane.
但在这一切之下，他其实只是一个害怕的小男孩，生活中没有男性的榜样，只是试图拼凑出一种成为男人的方式，而这种方式简直有点疯狂。

00:13:49.919 --> 00:13:52.480
But he also like, he ultimately has a good heart.
但他也，他最终有一颗善良的心。

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:55.399
And that's what he keeps seeing on this rewatch.
这就是他在这次重温中不断看到的东西。

00:13:55.399 --> 00:14:07.009
And I wrote you, this little ditty, to sing to you, in New York City, will be right back.
我为你写了这首小曲，要在纽约市唱给你听，我们马上回来。

00:14:07.009 --> 00:14:09.830
Hey, I'm Carl Radke.
嘿，我是卡尔·拉德克。

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:18.330
You might know me from Bravo's Summer House, but on my podcast, More Life, I get your favorite reality stars off the clock and into the conversations you don't usually get to hear.
你可能通过 Bravo 电视台的《夏日别墅》认识我，但在我的播客《更多生活》中，我让你们最喜欢的真人秀明星们卸下工作，进入那些你平时听不到的对话中。

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:21.730
The deep stuff, the vulnerable stuff, the uncomfortable stuff.
那些深刻的事情，那些脆弱的事情，那些让人不自在的事情。

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:27.649
I've sat down with Sierra Miller, Derinda Medley, Tom Schwartz, Lindsey Hubbard, and a whole lot more.
我曾与西拉·米勒、多琳达·梅德利、汤姆·施瓦茨、林赛·哈伯德以及更多人坐在一起畅谈。

00:14:27.649 --> 00:14:33.169
We get into relationships growing up, the messy stuff, and everything that happens when the cameras aren't rolling.
我们深入探讨了成长过程中的人际关系、那些一团糟的事情，以及摄像机没拍到时发生的一切。

00:14:33.169 --> 00:14:44.769
New episodes of More Life every week, listen wherever you get your podcasts or watch on YouTube.
每周都有《更多生活》的新节目，请在各大播客平台收听，或在 YouTube 上观看。

00:14:44.769 --> 00:14:46.690
And now, back to the show.
现在，让我们回到节目中。

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:59.960
I've always felt that he was kind of broken, and a lot of the show was, as the seasons went on was like revealing elements of his, you know, he never talked about his job.
我一直觉得他是那种破碎的人，随着季数的推进，节目的很大一部分就像是逐渐揭示他内心的元素，你知道的，他从未谈论过他的工作。

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:00.960
He never spoke.
他从不提及。

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:04.399
And as we're running gags of like, please.
随着我们不断玩梗，比如，拜托。

00:15:04.399 --> 00:15:06.639
And so you never really knew much about him.
所以你从不知道关于他太多的事。

00:15:06.639 --> 00:15:13.399
And that seemed like a good, a good place to hide sincerity and pay those.
这似乎是一个隐藏真诚和悲情的绝佳地方。

00:15:13.399 --> 00:15:15.600
And I keep in mind up and tell how I met your mother.
我记住并讲述了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:23.080
I hadn't done what I mean, I did a wacky sitcom with Tony Shalub on NBC a few years before how I met your mother.
我还没做过——我的意思是，在《老爸老妈的浪漫史》几年前，我在 NBC 和托尼·夏鲁卜拍过一部古怪的情景喜剧。

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:45.872
one season and I was like the David Hyde Piercy kind of afraid of germs and an uptight guy and so that was fun but sort of very singular but I had never really done a lot of the big comedy stuff so I had mostly done like Dewey Houser was super sincere and a lot of television movies that had been like I was grounded in sincerity.
只拍了一季，我扮演的是那种类似大卫·海德·皮尔斯的、有点洁癖且古板的家伙，所以那很有趣，但非常单一，而且我从未真正演过很多大型喜剧，所以大多数时候我都演过，比如《天才小医生》超级真诚，还有很多电视电影，我的表演都扎根于真诚。

00:15:45.872 --> 00:15:56.440
I feel like you had to do before Barney you did a lot of of what I had to do on How I Met Your Mother, which is kind of be the virtuous center of a show.
我觉得你在巴ney之前不得不做的事，很多就是我在《老爸老妈的浪漫史》里不得不做的事，那就是成为一个节目的道德中心。

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:02.120
And I felt there was something about, like you recognize the opportunity to be unleashed.
我觉得有种感觉，就像你意识到了被彻底释放的机会。

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:02.960
For sure.
那是肯定的。

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:11.279
To be like, oh my God, I, there was something about Barney, both the fact that he wore a lot of armor, like you're describing, like he had a lot of armor up.
就像，哦天哪，我，关于巴ney有一种感觉，既在于他穿着厚重的盔甲，就像你所描述的那样，他竖起了很多盔甲。

00:16:11.279 --> 00:16:14.120
The suit was like an armor for him.
西装就像是他的盔甲。

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.840
But he also, the way he was constructed and the way you played him.
但同时，他的构建方式以及你扮演他的方式。

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:35.559
And I, you know, I think we developed these characters with the writers, it was a real team effort, but the way you gave him permission to be as insane as a human could possibly be and still actually be recognizable as a human, I'm just very impressed with it on this rewatch.
而且我，你知道，我认为我们和编剧一起塑造了这些角色，这是一次真正的团队合作，但你赋予了他像人类一样疯狂、同时又真正能被认作是人类的许可，在这部重温中，我对此印象深刻。

00:16:35.559 --> 00:16:46.679
I appreciated it on set, but the more I watch it, and especially, this is something we talk about a lot, your physical comedy on the show is just unbelievable to watch.
我在片场就很欣赏这一点，但看得越多，尤其是——这是我们经常讨论的一点——你在剧中的肢体喜剧简直不可思议。

00:16:46.679 --> 00:16:52.159
I remember the marathon episode where he's like, I can just, where you don't train for a marathon, you just run it, right?
我记得马拉松那一集，他说，我完全可以——你根本不为马拉松进行训练，你直接去跑，对吧？

00:16:52.159 --> 00:16:54.320
And then his legs give out on the subway.
然后他的双腿在地铁里瘫软了。

00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.000
The shot that they showed was above the window, because it's like really funny you try to get up.
他们展示的镜头是从窗户上方拍的，因为你试图站起来的样子真的很好笑。

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:09.680
But I was watching, I was on set in the scene, and I'm watching through the door, so I actually saw your legs not work in a way that I was like, how did he do that?
但我当时在看，我在片场的那个场景中，我透过门看着，所以我实际上看到你的双腿完全使不上劲，当时我就想，他是怎么做到的？

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.960
Like, I didn't even understand, literally, physically.
就像我甚至都不理解，字面意义上，身体上。

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.839
You so believe you were about to stand up.
你太坚信自己马上要站起来了。

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:22.640
Yes, the top half of your body was doing one thing, and the bottom was doing something completely different.
对，你的上半身在做一件事，下半身却在做完全不同的事。

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:26.839
It got us thinking, Josh, I think we're both one of NASA's questions.
这让我们开始思考，乔斯，我觉得我们俩都是美国宇航局的某种研究对象。

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:30.559
We're just wondering, we so love your physical comedy.
我们只是在想，我们太喜欢你的肢体喜剧了。

00:17:30.559 --> 00:17:35.480
We were just wondering, did you grow up idolizing any physical comedians?
我们只是想知道，你成长过程中有崇拜过什么肢体喜剧演员吗？

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:37.039
Were you a Buster Keaton guy?
你是个巴斯特·基顿迷吗？

00:17:37.039 --> 00:17:39.960
I've never asked you this, and we're really curious.
我从来没问过你这个，我们真的很好奇。

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.319
A massive physical comedy fan.
一个超级伟大的肢体喜剧粉丝。

00:17:42.319 --> 00:17:48.213
Buster Keaton was a huge inspiration, Harold Lloyd, Charlie Chaplin.
巴斯特·基顿是一个巨大的灵感来源，还有哈罗德·劳埃德，查理·卓别林。

00:17:48.213 --> 00:17:58.960
But then it really pivoted when I went and saw the second ever version of Cirque du Soleil's traveling show in Santa Monica.
但后来当我去看太阳马戏团在圣莫尼卡的巡演的第二个版本时，情况真的发生了转变。

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.599
At the Santa Monica Pier and it was called the Novette ex-Pierliance, the New Experience.
在圣莫尼卡码头，它被称为 Novette ex-Pierliance，也就是“全新体验”。

00:18:06.319 --> 00:18:14.298
And it was the first time I had, well, it was the second time I'd seen Cirque because I'd seen their first show randomly there at that same spot.
那是我第一次，哦，其实是我第二次看太阳马戏，因为我之前在那同一个地方偶然看过他们的第一场演出。

00:18:14.298 --> 00:18:16.720
And I'd never seen a circus with no animals.
我从来没见过没有动物的马戏。

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:21.190
And it was surrounded by this central figure who was a clown.
而且它围绕着一个核心人物，他是一个小丑。

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:24.203
And the clown was a performer named David Shiner.
那个小丑是一个名叫大卫·沙纳（David Shiner）的表演者。

00:18:24.203 --> 00:18:26.908
And David Shiner is a very well-known clown.
大卫·沙纳是一个非常著名的小丑。

00:18:26.908 --> 00:18:31.150
And one of his main things that he did was he was played as director.
他做的主打事情之一就是扮演导演。

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:33.241
And he wouldn't talk as the clown.
他作为小丑是不会说话的。

00:18:33.241 --> 00:18:36.868
His clown spirit was very dismissive and annoyed at things.
他的小丑精神对很多事情都表现得非常不屑和恼火。

